    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate on 2026-08-15, **Corbin Carroll** remains our undisputed anchor with a massive 83.53 score against Grant Holmes. While Holmes carries a middling 4.65 xERA, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a strong lineup position (#2) and a favorable platoon advantage that vaults him into our elite tier despite a minor reduction for facing a pitcher with a decent hold rate against the run game. **Josh Naylor** (73.37) and **Seiya Suzuki** (67.75) also present high-floor, high-upside profiles; **Seiya Suzuki** is particularly well-positioned to capitalize on a positive park factor and a comfortable matchup against McGreevy, while **Josh Naylor** should thrive in the middle of our order against the shaky xERA of Wesneski.\n\nWe are keeping a close eye on the volatile matchup against MacKenzie Gore. While **Max Muncy** (55.64), **Henry Bolte** (48.54), and **Tyler Soderstrom** (57.87) are all currently projected to start, their numbers are buoyed by park factors rather than individual dominance over the southpaw. The decision to start **Brice Turang** (64.79) remains a high-conviction play; even with a slightly negative platoon effect, his proximity to the base-stealing \"green light\" against a slow-delivering pitcher and the benefit of a reported wind-out scenario makes him our premier speed threat for the day. **Travis Bazzana** (42.21) was the final player left on the cutting room floor; his projection was ultimately suppressed by a difficult L/L matchup against Wandy Peralta, leaving him as our primary bench pivot should any late lineup scratches occur. \n\nManaging risk is paramount today, as several players\u2014including **Josh Naylor**, **Cole Young**, **Max Muncy**, **Brice Turang**, **Austin Riley**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2014are currently tied to \"Lineup Pending\" designations. Should the official lineups shift these players into lower-priority slots, our efficiency scores will drift toward the marginal range. 